@page "/components/ai/attachmentchip"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/attachmentchip"
            Title="AiAttachmentChip"
            Description="BitAiAttachmentChip shows one attachment of an AI chat as a chip: a thumbnail or the glyph of its kind, its name, and its size, the progress of its upload or why it failed." />

<DemoPage Name="AiAttachmentChip"
          SecondaryNames="@(["FileChip"])"
          Description="BitAiAttachmentChip shows one BitAiAttachment as a chip - a BitTag: a thumbnail for an image and the glyph of its kind for anything else, its name, and under it its size, the progress of an upload still arriving, or why the upload failed. Its dismiss button removes the attachment, there only while OnRemove is set. The composer stages files as these chips, and a message shows its files with them."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          PublicMembers="componentPublicMembers"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="AttachmentChip/BitAiAttachmentChip.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/AttachmentChip/BitAiAttachmentChipDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The chip only shows a <b>BitAiAttachment</b> - it reads no file and needs no service. The
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/promptinput">BitAiPromptInput</BitLink> makes one per staged file, and a
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/chatmessage">BitAiChatMessage</BitLink> one per file of the message; see the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/attachments">attachments scenario</BitLink> for both. A thumbnail is decoration beside the
            name (an empty alt), and the remove button is named after the file (Remove quarterly-report.pdf). Only an image is
            drawn from its own content, and only as the image policy in force (<b>BitAiUiOptions.Images</b>) allows, so point
            <b>ThumbnailUrl</b> or <b>Url</b> only at an image you trust to be one. Its texts - the Remove of the button, the
            UploadFailed of a failed upload without an <b>Error</b>, and the formatted size (<b>FormatBytes</b>) - come from the
            strings in force, which a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/optionsprovider">BitAiOptionsProvider</BitLink> localizes.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Give it an <b>Attachment</b>: the chip shows its name, its size under it, and the glyph of its kind. A name too long
                for the chip is cut short and is its tooltip.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="report" />
            <br /><br />
            <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="longName" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Kinds" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                The <b>Kind</b> of an attachment picks what stands before its name: an image shows its <b>ThumbnailUrl</b> (or its
                <b>Url</b>), audio, video and any other file the glyph of their kind. <b>BitAiAttachment.InferKind</b> works the kind
                out of a MIME type and a file name, the way the composer does.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem">
                @foreach (var attachment in kinds)
                {
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip @key="attachment.AttachmentId" Attachment="attachment" />
                }
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Image policy" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                The picture of an image follows <b>BitAiUiOptions.Images</b>, the
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview#image-policy">BitAiImagePolicy</BitLink> every image of a chat follows,
                cascaded here by a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/optionsprovider">BitAiOptionsProvider</BitLink>. A chip is too small
                for a placeholder, so an image the policy does not show outright - held back by <b>ClickToLoad</b> or a host
                <b>AllowListedHosts</b> does not list, or hidden by <b>Block</b> - gets the glyph of its kind instead: the first chip
                is under the default policy, the second under <b>Block</b>. An address with a script-capable scheme is never drawn,
                whatever the policy.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem">
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="photo" />
                <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="blockImagesOptions">
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="photo" />
                </BitAiOptionsProvider>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Status" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                The <b>Status</b> of an upload shows under the name: the size while it is <b>Pending</b> - press the button to send
                it - then the <b>Progress</b> of one still <b>Uploading</b>, and the size again once it is <b>Ready</b>; the
                <b>Error</b> of one that <b>Failed</b> (the UploadFailed text of the strings in force when it has none) shows in
                the error color, the state of the file rather than a look of the chip.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton OnClick="UploadAsync" Disabled="isUploading">Upload a file</BitButton>
            <br /><br />
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem">
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="upload" />
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="failed" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="OnRemove" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>OnRemove</b> adds the dismiss button of the tag, named after the file, and hands over the attachment it removes;
                without it the chip has no button at all. The button goes with its chip, so the focus is handed on with
                <b>FocusAsync</b>: to the chip that took its place, or the last one, or the button that brings them back.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem">
                @foreach (var attachment in staged)
                {
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip @key="attachment.AttachmentId" @ref="stagedChips[attachment.AttachmentId]" Attachment="attachment" OnRemove="Remove" />
                }
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitButton @ref="resetButton" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="ResetStaged">Bring them back</BitButton>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                A disabled chip is dimmed and its remove button cannot be pressed.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiAttachmentChip Disabled Attachment="report" OnRemove="RemoveDisabled" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiAttachmentChipParams</b> sets the look of every attachment chip inside it - its
                <b>Styles</b>, <b>Classes</b> and the common parameters such as <b>Dir</b> or <b>Disabled</b>; the attachment and
                <b>OnRemove</b> stay on each chip. A parameter of the chip itself still wins: the last one sets its own <b>Styles</b>.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitParams Parameters="attachmentChipParams">
                <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem">
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="report" />
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="photo" />
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="longName" Styles="@(new() { Thumbnail = "border-radius:0" })" />
                </div>
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> the tag (BitTagClassStyles), the
                thumbnail and the glyph. The public <b>--bit-AiAttachmentChip-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) size the
                chip and its thumbnail, from <b>:root</b>, an ancestor or <b>Style</b>; the rest of the chip is a BitTag, restyled
                through its own <b>--bit-Tag-*</b> variables.
            </div>
            <br />
            @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
            <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start;gap:0.75rem">
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="report" Style="padding:0.25rem;border:1px dashed var(--bit-clr-brd-pri)" />
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="photo" Class="custom-class" />
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="report"
                                     Styles="@(new() { Tag = new() { Root = "border-radius:1rem" }, Thumbnail = "border-radius:50%", Icon = "color:var(--bit-clr-pri)" })" />
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="photo"
                                     Classes="@(new() { Tag = new() { Text = "custom-text" }, Thumbnail = "custom-thumbnail" })" />
                <div style="@cssVariablesStyle">
                    <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="longName" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example9CsharpCode" Id="example9">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the thumbnail stands at the right of the name and the remove button at its left, whether the
                direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around the chip.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiAttachmentChip Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Attachment="persianReport" OnRemove="RemoveRtl" />
            <br /><br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitAiAttachmentChip Attachment="persianPhoto" OnRemove="RemoveRtl" />
            </div>
            <br />
            <div>Removed: <b>@rtlRemoved</b></div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
